  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nA somewhat complex pattern is in place to start off the week as a \nnegatively tilted 500mb trough over the north-central Plains forces \nsurface low pressure SE across the Red River valley toward the Gulf. \nMeanwhile, mid-level ridging extends from MO north through MN, with \nsurface high pressure centered over eastern MO. This high pressure \nwill keep us dry and will help lift highs into the low-mid 50s \ntoday, which is seasonable for mid-November. In fact, the forecast \nhigh today in Kansas City is 54F, which happens to be the normal \nhigh temperature for this date. Cloud cover will be limited this \nafternoon due to subsidence associated with the high pressure, and \nclear skies tonight will allow for efficient radiational cooling \novernight. Most locations will fall into the low 30s; however, a few \nisolated sheltered areas may get down into the mid-upper 20s. \n\nHigh pressure and southerly surface flow continue to dictate our \nsensible weather heading into the workweek, with highs Monday once \nagain in the mid-upper 50s. Lows Monday night into Tuesday morning \nwill be 5-8 degrees warmer than the previous night. By Tuesday \nmorning, an upper-level shortwave will have come ashore on the West \nCoast. As this system begins to cross the Intermountain West on \nTuesday afternoon, the pressure gradient will tighten across the \nPlains. This will drive sustained southerly surface winds up into \nthe 10-15mph range, which will only further increase high \ntemperatures Tuesday and Wednesday. By Wednesday, most locations \nwill hit 60F, with some spots in SW MO approaching 70F. \n\nThe aforementioned lead shortwave ejects into the northern \nPlains Wednesday evening, but questions remain concerning \nconfidence in rainfall across the region. The strongest forcing \nwith this system will be confined to the upper-Midwest, and \nsouthwesterly surface flow will allow for dry air to permeate \nthe lower levels across the area. That being said, if lower \nlevels are able to saturate, isolated to scattered showers will \nbe possible Wednesday evening into the overnight for areas along\nand north of the MO River, where PoPs range from 15-30%. Where \nrain does fall, accumulations will be light, with the 75th \npercentile for accumulated precipitation between .05-.1\". \n\nBy the end of the week, highs will be in the upper 60s to low 70s, \nwhich is well above normal for this time of year. Attention then \nturns to yet another trough that will impact the Midwest heading \ninto the next weekend. Long-range guidance shows a strong sub-1000mb \nlow and attendant cold front translating across the upper-midwest \nFriday evening through Saturday. This is our best chance for \nwidespread rainfall in the next seven days, with PoPs of 40-60% in \nplace for much of the region. We are still too far out at this point \nto resolve the finer details of the system, but instability and \nforcing currently look limited across our area, which would decrease \nthunderstorm potential. If this system ejects further south than \ncurrently forecast, then thunderstorm potential would increase. Will \ncontinue to monitor forecast trends as we get closer in time to the \nweekend.",
